From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@conda-forge.org>
Date: Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: umd: do not append -g to Release builds

umd/CMakeLists.txt unconditionally appends -g to the Release flags:

    set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE   "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE} -g")
    set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE} -g")

The top-level CMakeLists.txt defaults CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE to Release, so an
ordinary build is really RelWithDebInfo: libze_intel_npu.so is built with
full DWARF, which dominates its installed size wherever the packaging does
not strip it, and the LTO link has to merge all of that debug info. On
x86_64 with GCC 14 the installed library measures 21.3 MB with the -g, and
1.6 MB without it.

CMake already has a build type that means "optimised, with debug info".
Anyone wanting debug symbols can select -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo
explicitly, which is both discoverable and overridable; silently turning
Release into something else is neither. The sections are non-ALLOC, so
they are never mapped at runtime and add nothing to a distributed runtime
package.

Replace the -g additions with a comment pointing at RelWithDebInfo, and
let the Release optimisation flags stand on their own.

Signed-off-by: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@conda-forge.org>

---
 umd/CMakeLists.txt | ~
 1 file changed
diff --git a/umd/CMakeLists.txt b/umd/CMakeLists.txt
index 1e0de53..ccd2004 100644
--- a/umd/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/umd/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -34,8 +34,8 @@ else()
     add_compile_options(-Wno-unused-but-set-variable -Wno-unused-variable)
 endif()
 
-set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE} -g")
-set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE} -g")
+# Release builds are optimised without debug info; select the
+# RelWithDebInfo build type if debug symbols are wanted.
 
 set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG} -DUMD_LOGLEVEL=${UMD_LOGLEVEL}")
 
